1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,110
In 1925, a man sold the Eiffel Tower.

2
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:07,450
Then he came back, and tried to sell it again.

3
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:10,090
His name was Victor Lustig.

4
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:12,930
And he never owned a single bolt of it.

5
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:16,410
As the story goes, it began with a newspaper.

6
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:20,050
The tower was rusting, and repairs were expensive.

7
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:22,130
Lustig saw an opportunity.

8
00:00:22,170 --> 00:00:29,570
He printed official-looking letters, and invited scrap dealers to a secret meeting, in a grand Paris hotel.

9
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:35,430
Posing as a government official, he said the city would sell the tower for scrap.

10
00:00:35,470 --> 00:00:38,630
7,000 tons of iron. Top secret.

11
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:42,990
One dealer, André Poisson, wanted it badly.

12
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:46,790
He was new in Paris, and wanted to be someone.

13
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:51,170
But he had doubts. Why so secret? Why so fast?

14
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:55,530
So he confessed. Officials were badly paid.

15
00:00:55,570 --> 00:00:57,370
He wanted a bribe.

16
00:00:57,550 --> 00:00:58,710
And that convinced him.

17
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:02,070
Only a real official would ask for a bribe.

18
00:01:02,750 --> 00:01:05,690
Poisson paid for the tower, and the bribe.

19
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:08,810
Lustig took the money, and left the country.

20
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:12,690
And Poisson? He was too embarrassed to tell the police.

21
00:01:14,050 --> 00:01:17,130
So Lustig came back, and tried to sell it again.

22
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:21,130
This time, someone called the police. He slipped away.

23
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:25,930
Years later, he was caught in America, for printing fake money.

24
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:30,090
The tower is still standing. Still not for sale.

